    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hi, I'm trying to get a handle on some inheritance tax issues for my family. We have assets spread across the UK and a few other countries, and it's getting pretty complicated. Can you explain how tax residency usually works when determining which country has the right to tax an inherited estate?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Tax residency is primarily established by where an individual lives and, in many jurisdictions, by whether they spend more than 160 days (for the UK) or 183 days (for the US) in that country within a 12-month period. If you are considered a tax resident, that country generally has the right to tax your worldwide assets, while other countries where you do not reside usually cannot tax inherited assets unless they have specific laws regarding \"domicile\" rather than residency. To manage this complexity, it is often necessary to verify your exact residency status in each country involved to determine which jurisdiction claims the inheritance tax.",
      "source": "target",
